  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "The short answer",
      "items": [
        "A key holder's click creates a random 26-character token tied to one file id",
        "The guest page and its download go through three public routes that only accept such a token",
        "the router answers them before the access-key gate, and every other API route still demands a session, so the token can name one file and nothing else"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "slate",
      "title": "Not shown here",
      "items": [
        "The browsers, Cloudflare and the tunnel, which carry a guest's request exactly as in the overview",
        "the Workspace app, where a key holder creates the link with a normal signed-in request",
        "Connected but out of scope: cloudflared tunnel, Chunked uploads, Zip streamer, Expiry sweeper, Env file, Auto-deploy timer"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "rose",
      "title": "What it guarantees",
      "items": [
        "A guest token opens one file and nothing else: the only unauthenticated routes are /d/<token>, /d/<token>/download and /api/guest/<token>; every other /api route hits the 401 gate, and the token resolves to a single file id",
        "Cloudflare never caches a file: download URLs carry no file extension and every API response is Cache-Control: private, no-store"
      ]
    }
  ]
